  "worked_solution": "Since $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >3 \\\\ x-1 & >2 \\\\ |x-1| & >2 \\\\ |x-1|-1 & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $|x-1|-1$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>3).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $|x-1|-1,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (|x-1|-1)\\cdot \\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1} & \\ge (|x-1|-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 8-2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -8 \\\\ x & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality again since we divided by a negative number ($-2$).\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>3$ and $x\\le 4.$ So, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\n3<x\\le 4.\n$$",
